From f2303f95e34f80b244826cf9d7d7600d8c359906 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 07:14:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: the same mastering display in another form makes
 no new renderer, and every stream keeps the static metadata it carried

Whether two pictures have the same mastering display decides whether the
renderer is made again (VideoPicture::IsSameParams(),
CRenderManager::Configure()), which sets the output up again: the display
is told the metadata again, and native Dolby Vision output ends and starts
again. CompareDisplayMetadata() compared the numerators and denominators
of the luminances, and did not look at has_luminance. The same display
comes in more than one form: FFmpeg gives the SEI of HEVC as fractions of
its steps (10000000/10000 for 1000 cd/m2), Matroska's demuxer gives its
floats as av_d2q() makes them (1000/1, and 5368709/1073741824 for a
minimum of 0.005 cd/m2 stored in four bytes). An HDR10 stream whose
pictures had the SEI and then had the container's metadata made the
renderer again at each change: a stutter, and what the display is told
changed in the middle of the video.

They are now compared as the bitstream and the HDMI InfoFrame code them:
the luminances in steps of 0.0001 cd/m2, the primaries and the white
point in steps of 0.00002 (an exact comparison of the fractions would
still tell the float of 0.005 from the SEI's 50/10000). Both pictures
have a luminance or neither has, and the values are compared only where
both have them; the same for the primaries and white point, which go to
the display with the luminance (SetHDR(), only when the renderer is
configured) and were not compared at all. The content light levels are
still not compared, as in Kodi: they change from one clip of a Blu-ray
playlist to the next, and making the renderer again there would end
native output in the middle of a title. Each picture costs one comparison
of the fractions, as before; their values are worked out only where the
fractions differ. (HdrStaticMetadata.h)

The SEI of HEVC applies to the coded layer video sequence of its picture
(H.265: it persists to the end of the CLVS, and all that apply to one
CLVS have the same content). FFmpeg 9.0.2 gives it to every picture of
that sequence and to none of the next one that does not carry it again
(hevcdec.c counts its presence down at each IRAP picture with
NoRaslOutputFlag set), and forgets it at every flush (a seek). An encoder
that carries it on the first IRAP picture only left every picture from
the next IDR picture on, and every picture after a seek, with the
container's metadata, or none. A stream set up for native Dolby Vision
output or for the reconstruction kept the last that it carried in band;
every stream does now (CInBandHdrMetadata), in the decoder of FFmpeg and
in the DRM PRIME one: a picture without it takes the last that the stream
carried with the same transfer function, a new SEI replaces it, the
container's is used before any SEI has come, and nothing is kept from one
stream to the next. It is kept when the decoder of the same stream is
reset or opened again.

The idea of comparing the luminances by value is CroqueMr's
(libreelec-x86-DV, preview/rc4-update3, 5145be51ae, "Compare HDR mastering
luminance by rational value"); written for this tree.
